{"step_index":173,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:36:12Z","thinking":"**Proving Total Domination Number Lower Bound**\n\nIf a set `S` is a total dominating set for graph `G`, then it must have a cardinality of at least 2. The case of a set with 0 elements is impossible because `G`'s vertex set is nontrivial.\nIf `S` has a cardinality of 1, the single vertex cannot be totally dominating; its only potential neighbor would be itself, leading to a contradiction.\nThus, any total dominating set `S` has at least cardinality of 2.\n\n\n**Exploring Radius-Domination Bound**\n\nThe previous hypothesis of `radius G ≤ 2` failed, as demonstrated by the counterexample of P6 (path graph with 6 vertices). This necessitates finding a more general approach to proving the radius is less than or equal to the total domination number.\nAttempting to connect this problem with `Metric.lean`'s `radius` definitions and `dist` relations within Mathlib. Also, reviewing mathematical proof for the radius being bounded by total domination number.\nFocusing on how a total dominating set relates to the definition of the radius in a graph.\n\n\n**Developing Domination Number Theorem**\n\nConstructing a formal proof for the theorem relating total domination and vertex distances. The components of the induced subgraph G[S] were found to each contain at least two vertices.\nDeducing the number of components is at most half the size of the total dominating set. Analyzing the spanning tree of the component-contracted graph to relate the maximum distance.\nConsidering the upper bound of the tree radius on the number of components and component diameters within the original graph.\n\n\n**Considering Induction Strategies**\n\nInitially, attempting to formalize a complex proof involving component contraction and diameters appears overly difficult. Investigating induction on the size of the total dominating set `S` or on the graph `G` itself, as potential simplification strategies.\nThe base case for |S| = 2 seems trivial to